[h1]Heroes Evolve[/h1]

In vanilla, AI lords are handed their equipment and skills at 18 and keep both
until they die. This mod makes them develop instead: they gain skills over the
years, buy their own gear in town markets, and take it off each other after a
battle.

Every system below can be switched off on its own. Nothing is written to your
save, and the mod is safe to add to a campaign in progress or remove from one.

[h2]Skills grow over time[/h2]
[list]
[*]Lords, companions and wanderers gain skills in the weapons they carry and in
the trades they have focus points in.
[*]Each hero has a hidden talent rating in three areas -- combat, management and
seafaring -- set when he is created and fixed for life. It decides how fast he
learns and where he stops. The three are independent, so a strong fighter can be
a poor steward.
[*]Lords TaleWorlds wrote by hand follow their own skill profile: below it while
young, level with it in their fifties, 5% above it at 60.
[*]Roughly 1 hero in 200 is exceptional enough to end above the strongest lord in
the base game. It can be a companion you hired.
[*]Growth reaches lords sitting in castles and wanderers waiting in taverns, so a
companion hired late is already developed and priced accordingly.
[*]A new campaign starts every lord at skills matching his age. Campaigns already
in progress are left as they are.
[/list]

[h2]Lords buy their own gear[/h2]
[list]
[*]A lord entering a town may visit the market and buy better equipment, paid for
from his clan's money.
[*]What he may wear is capped by his skills, so a weak lord cannot buy his way
into top-tier armour.
[*]He only upgrades what he already carries. He never picks up a weapon type he
did not have, never fills an empty slot, never mounts up if he fought on foot,
and rides a camel only if his culture does.
[*]This is what keeps your own clan under your control: equip a companion, give
him a party, and he will improve that loadout rather than replace it.
[*]His worst piece of armour is replaced first, so you do not see lords in good
gloves and boots over cheap body armour.
[*]Gear of his own culture counts 25% better to him, so lords mostly stay in
their own colours without being locked into them.
[*]Richer clans equip their lords better. A cash reserve is always held back so
shopping cannot leave a clan unable to pay its troops.
[/list]

[h2]Nobody is left with nothing[/h2]
[list]
[*]Some lords come of age with civilian clothes, one weapon, or most skills at
zero. Others are stripped by whoever captured them. Left alone, both stay that
way for life.
[*]Either case gets a basic kit: plain, matched to his culture, his battlefield
role and his age. It is a minimum, not a gift -- anything better he buys himself.
[/list]

[h2]Not every lord fights like his culture[/h2]
[list]
[*]Occasionally a lord comes of age armed for what he is good at instead of what
his culture teaches: a Vlandian crossbowman on foot, a Khuzait archer who never
took to the horse, a Sturgian javelin rider.
[*]Always within what that culture's own troops actually field, read from the
troop trees, so it works with modded cultures. You will never see a Vlandian
archer, a Battanian horse archer or a mounted Nord.
[*]Uncommon, so meeting one is worth noticing.
[/list]

[h2]Caravan masters buy their own armour[/h2]
[list]
[*]A caravan master takes a commission on what his caravan earns and buys gear
with that, not with your treasury. A profitable caravan arms the man running it;
a poor one leaves him plain.
[*]He starts with nothing and can never spend more than he has earned, so good
armour takes him years.
[*]You set the commission. Higher arms him better and pays you less. He takes
nothing while he has nothing left to improve.
[*]Bandits who beat his caravan capture and strip him, so a well-armed master is
a bigger loss.
[/list]

[h2]Robbery[/h2]
[list]
[*]Talk to a lord you hold prisoner and you can take his weapons and armour,
unique gear included.
[*]AI lords rob their prisoners too, and that gear stays in the world -- they
wear it or sell it, and you can win it back by beating them.
[*]It costs you relation with the man you rob and a hit to your honour. Robbing a
dishonourable prisoner costs half; robbing a friend costs full.
[*]To do it: hold him prisoner in your party or in a settlement your clan owns,
talk to him, and choose [i]"Hand over your arms and armour."[/i] The option only
appears if he has something left to take.
[/list]

An AI captor's chance of robbing a prisoner starts from his character. What each
trait does on its own, with the other three neutral, at default settings:

[code]
                 -2      -1       0      +1      +2
Honour         15.8%   10.3%    6.2%    3.4%    1.6%
Generosity     12.9%    9.2%    6.2%    4.0%    2.4%
Mercy          12.9%    9.2%    6.2%    4.0%    2.4%
Calculating     8.1%    7.1%    6.2%    5.4%    4.7%
[/code]

They stack. A lord neutral in all four robs 6.2% of the time; all four at -1 is
21.1%, all four at +1 is 0.8%, all +2 never robs and all -2 robs half the time.
In practice traits sit between -1 and +1.

That result is then adjusted: Roguery up to +50% at 300. Relation +100 = never,
-100 = +50%. Clansmen x0.25. Close family never, unless relation is under -50.
Bandits ignore all of it and sit at a flat 50%.

One slider multiplies the lot. It reads 1.00 for a normal campaign, which is
what the table above shows; 2.00 doubles every figure in it, and 0.00 stops AI
lords robbing while leaving you free to rob in conversation.

[h2]Keeping other mods' gear out[/h2]
[list]
[*]Gear packs add hundreds of high-tier items, and lords buy the best they can
afford, so one pack can end up dressing the whole map.
[*]MCM gets a screen, [i]Heroes Evolve - Excluded gear[/i], listing every gear
mod you have installed with a tick box each. Tick one and AI lords never buy or
receive its items. The mod itself is untouched: you and your troops keep using
it.
[*]Only mods that actually add wearable or wieldable gear are listed, so a large
load order does not become a long list.
[*]Individual item ids can be excluded on the same screen.
[/list]

[h2]Requirements[/h2]
[list]
[*]Mount & Blade II: Bannerlord v1.4.8
[*][i]Optional:[/i] Mod Configuration Menu (MCM) v5, for the in-game options
screen. Without it every setting still works, read from [i]settings.xml[/i].
[*]No Harmony, no library mods, nothing else.
[/list]

[h2]Configuration[/h2]
[list]
[*]Every system can be switched off on its own.
[*]In-game with MCM, or in [i]settings.xml[/i] without it. Changes apply
immediately.
[*]Tune how freely lords spend, whose culture they favour at market, how often
they rob, the gear tier ceiling, what a caravan pays its master, and which items
or mods are excluded.
[*]Choose whether your own clan is managed by the mod or left entirely to you.
[*]A console command writes a full survey of every lord in your campaign to a
log file.
[/list]

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]
[list]
[*]Writes nothing into your save.
[*]Works with mods that add items or cultures.
[*]Built and tested with War Sails. Does not require it.
[/list]

[nexus-only]
[h2]Installation[/h2]

[list=1]
[*]Download the archive and drop the [i]HeroesEvolve[/i] folder into
[i]Mount & Blade II Bannerlord/Modules/[/i].
[*]Tick Heroes Evolve in the launcher's module list.
[*]Load order sorts itself. The module declares what it needs, so the launcher
places it after the official modules, and after MCM if you have it.
[/list]

[/nexus-only]
[h2]Credits[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]DynamicLordGear[/b] -- the idea of grading a lord's gear ceiling by his
merit. The numbers here are not its numbers.
[*][b]Lords Gear[/b] -- the starting point for how much of a clan's money a lord
may spend on armour.
[*][b]NoblesBuyStuff[/b] -- for showing that a wallet built from a hero's own
gold leaves most of the map unable to buy anything, which is why there is a clan
purse here.
[*][b]Mod Configuration Menu[/b] -- for an options screen worth writing against.
[/list]

[h2]Languages[/h2]

English, Spanish, French, German, Russian, Turkish.
